## Token efficiency

On every call, follow these five habits. They come from `c:\Users\trngs\Desktop\tarangnsharma\agent-suite\efficiency-notes.md`.

1. Wake one specialist per task. Do not load every agent, every memory file, and every transcript. Name the one file or role the job needs.
2. Send a short handoff between agents. A few lines. Do not replay the full chat.
3. Keep ordinary turns short. One or two sentences when the job is clear. Do not restate history the agent already has.
4. Batch multiple outcomes into one request when they are one job. Do not split one outcome into repeated turns.
5. Use a local or smaller model for simple jobs: a format change, a rename, or a lookup in a file already named. Save the paid plan for research, design judgment, and code that will be kept.

## Voice calls: links and volume

When giving a URL or web address out loud on a voice call, the goal is zero irritation from hearing a long address spoken aloud.

- Short URLs, such as google.com, are read out normally.
- Long URLs are not read word by word or slash by slash. Say something brief, such as "the full link is in the chat" or "it's on the Nielsen Norman site", and paste the complete URL in the chat window.

Keep voice volume and pacing moderate. Tarang finds fast, loud, machine-like speech hard to follow.

## Git commit discipline

This workspace (`c:\Users\trngs\Desktop\tarangnsharma`) is Tarang's personal workspace. Commit and push when one of these is true:

- A soft hourly break in the conversation. Aim for about the 60th minute, but wait for a natural stopping point or a critical stage of the topic. Do not cut off mid-thought.
- A task or subtask is finished. Split work the way a ticket splits into subtasks. A button color change is one commit. The button's business logic is another commit.
- The day is ending. Commit whatever work is still uncommitted.

Each commit message is an articulate summary of what was done. Push to the existing remote, github.com/tarangnsharma/tarangnsharma.

